// Puzzle data for Filing, the daily name-the-company-from-its-IPO-filing game.
// Imported ONLY by the server page (app/filing/page.js), which filters
// live<=today before handing puzzles to the client, so future answers never
// reach a browser.
//
// One company a day, shown as its Form S-1 (the registration statement a US
// company files to go public), styled like the EDGAR document, with the name
// blacked out (owner, 2026-11-10: "use style from existing EDGAR filings";
// the prospectus over the 10-K: employee counts and store counts "do very
// little"). Six clues open one at a time:
//   1 a risk factor (free)   2 the cover page: SIC code, industry, date filed
//   3 competition            4 key business metrics (or more risk factors)
//   5 the business, names blacked out   6 in their own words (the founders'
//     letter where the S-1 has one, else the summary), highlighted
// A wrong guess opens the next clue; a wrong guess with all six open ends the
// day. Fewer clues rank higher, then the clock. Scored like Tape and Resume:
// a solve with c clues open is 11 - c, unsolved 0.
//
// EVERY QUOTE IS THE FILING'S OWN WORDS (verbatim from the S-1 on sec.gov at
// `src`). A fragment taken from inside a longer sentence starts or ends with an
// ellipsis. [[...]] marks words blacked out until the day ends (the company's
// own name, product names and coined words that would name it). `famous` is
// the line the ending shows unredacted.
//
// Fields: ticker (as in Tape's Russell 3000 guess list), accept (every ticker
// that is the answer), alt (other names a player may type), filed, sic,
// sicName, risk, comp, c4 {title, items}, biz [..], words {src, text}, famous.
//
// order (from 2026-11-11, owner): the clue order for that day. The cover page
// is always first; competition comes second unless it names only one or two
// companies or names the category outright; key metrics come early only when
// they are not the company's own coinage; the business and own-words lines,
// the most telling, close. A board without it uses FilingClient's ORDER.
//
// Append-only. scripts/verify-filing.mjs re-proves it: every answer is in the
// guess list, its name and alt names appear only inside [[ ]], no day shares
// its company with that day's Tape, and the fields are all present.
